# IMO 2023 N4 — Solution

**Notation.** For $i = 0, 1, \ldots, n$ define
$$P_i \;=\; b_0 b_1 \cdots b_{i-1}\, a_i\, a_{i+1}\cdots a_{n-1},$$
with the empty products interpreted as $1$ (so $P_0 = a_0 a_1\cdots a_{n-1}$ and $P_n = b_0 b_1\cdots b_{n-1}$). The given condition (for $i = 0,1,\ldots,n-1$) reads
$$P_{i+1} = P_i + D.$$
Hence $P_0, P_1, \ldots, P_n$ is an arithmetic progression with common difference $D$; write
$$Q_i := P_i = Q_0 + iD, \qquad i = 0,\ldots,n.$$

Since $P_{i+1}/P_i = b_i/a_i$, we have
$$b_i = a_i\,\frac{Q_{i+1}}{Q_i} = a_i\,\frac{Q_i+D}{Q_i} = a_i + \frac{a_i D}{Q_i}.$$
Thus $b_i\in\mathbb N^+$ iff $Q_i \mid a_i D$. Also $\prod_{i=0}^{n-1} a_i = Q_0$ (telescoping: $\prod b_i/\prod a_i = Q_n/Q_0$, and $\prod b_i = Q_n$).

---

## Upper bound: $D = n!$ is attainable

Take
$$a_i = i+1,\qquad b_i = i+2 \quad (0\le i\le n-1),\qquad D = n!.$$
Then
$$P_i = \prod_{j=0}^{i-1}(j+2)\;\prod_{j=i}^{n-1}(j+1) = (i+1)!\cdot\frac{n!}{i!} = n!(i+1),$$
so $P_{i+1}-P_i = n! = D$ for every $i$. All entries are positive integers. Hence $D\le n!$.

---

## Lower bound: $D \ge n!$

Let $g = \gcd(Q_0, D)$ and write $Q_0 = gq$, $D = gd$ with $\gcd(q,d)=1$. Then
$$Q_i = g(q+id), \qquad i = 0,\ldots,n.$$

The divisibility $Q_i \mid a_i D$ becomes $g(q+id)\mid a_i\cdot gd$, i.e.
$$(q+id)\mid a_i d.$$
Since $\gcd(q+id,\,d)=\gcd(q,d)=1$, we conclude
$$(q+id)\mid a_i \qquad\text{for every } i=0,\ldots,n-1.$$

Multiplying over $i$ and using $\prod_{i=0}^{n-1} a_i = Q_0 = gq$:
$$\prod_{i=0}^{n-1}(q+id) \;\Big|\; gq.$$
Since the $i=0$ factor equals $q$, we may cancel it (as $q>0$):
$$\prod_{i=1}^{n-1}(q+id) \;\Big|\; g.$$
In particular
$$g \;\ge\; \prod_{i=1}^{n-1}(q+id).$$
Because $q\ge 1$ and $d\ge 1$, each factor satisfies $q+id \ge 1+i = i+1$, whence
$$g \;\ge\; \prod_{i=1}^{n-1}(i+1) = 2\cdot 3\cdots n = n!.$$
Finally
$$D = gd \ge g \ge n!$$
(since $d\ge 1$).

---

Combining both bounds, the smallest possible value of $D$ is

$$\boxed{D = n!}.$$

